WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.060
<v Maya>The textbooks are lying to you.</v>

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:03.380
<v Marcus>That's a big claim.</v>

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:04.560
<v Marcus>Which textbooks?</v>

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.560
<v Maya>I'm Maya, he's Marcus, and this is Chief of Staff Confidential.</v>

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:11.460
<v Maya>The change management ones.</v>

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.360
<v Maya>Unfreeze, change, refreeze.</v>

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:15.640
<v Maya>Eight steps.</v>

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:20.000
<v Maya>Start with urgency, build the coalition, never skip ahead.</v>

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:21.960
<v Maya>It all fits on one slide.</v>

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:26.460
<v Maya>So why does every real change I've ever watched ignore the slide completely?</v>

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.280
<v Marcus>Because the slide describes an organization.</v>

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.840
<v Marcus>And organizations don't change.</v>

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:39.440
<v Marcus>People do, one at a time, usually in private, and almost never in the order your plan needs.</v>

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:42.240
<v Marcus>Let me tell you about the merger that taught me that.</v>

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:43.000
<v Maya>Go on.</v>

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:45.600
<v Marcus>Two divisions being folded into one.</v>

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:48.880
<v Marcus>I had the plan, and the plan was beautiful.</v>

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:54.660
<v Marcus>Timeline, milestones, owners, a steering committee, a comms calendar.</v>

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:57.400
<v Marcus>The CEO announced it on a Monday.</v>

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:00.040
<v Marcus>By Friday every box was green.</v>

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:03.780
<v Marcus>And underneath the green, nothing had moved.</v>

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:04.860
<v Maya>Nothing?</v>

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:08.260
<v Marcus>The systems team was still approving work the old way.</v>

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:13.260
<v Marcus>The sales teams were still keeping two sets of numbers, theirs and ours.</v>

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:18.920
<v Marcus>And the whole thing ran through one man: the operations lead on the acquired side.</v>

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:20.860
<v Marcus>Twenty years in that division.</v>

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:27.620
<v Marcus>Every request I sent him came back answered, polite, complete, and quietly routed around.</v>

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:29.900
<v Marcus>On paper he was complying.</v>

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:35.080
<v Marcus>In practice the old division was still running, with him at the switch.</v>

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:36.620
<v Maya>So what did you do first?</v>

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:37.880
<v Marcus>The wrong thing.</v>

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:39.320
<v Marcus>I sent a better memo.</v>

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.600
<v Marcus>Clearer rationale, tighter deadlines, the CEO's name in the second paragraph.</v>

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:46.560
<v Marcus>He replied within the hour.</v>

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:47.820
<v Marcus>Very supportive.</v>

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:49.320
<v Marcus>Changed nothing.</v>

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:50.320
<v Maya>Of course.</v>

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:53.240
<v Marcus>That's when I learned the first real lesson of the job.</v>

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:55.920
<v Marcus>His resistance was never about the plan.</v>

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.980
<v Marcus>You cannot fix a person problem with a document.</v>

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:00.340
<v Maya>Then what was it about?</v>

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:03.940
<v Marcus>I finally stopped sending things and went and sat in his office.</v>

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:05.060
<v Marcus>No deck.</v>

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.940
<v Marcus>I asked him what the merger looked like from his chair.</v>

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:10.220
<v Marcus>And it came out in about four minutes.</v>

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:12.620
<v Marcus>He had built the system we were replacing.</v>

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:16.260
<v Marcus>Built it himself, over twenty years, fix by fix.</v>

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:23.060
<v Marcus>Every message we'd sent about the new way said the same thing to him: your twenty years were the problem we're solving.</v>

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:25.480
<v Marcus>He wasn't resisting the change.</v>

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:31.420
<v Marcus>He was defending his life's work, and we'd never once said out loud that the old system was good.</v>

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.220
<v Marcus>It had carried the company for two decades.</v>

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:36.660
<v Marcus>It just couldn't carry what came next.</v>

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:40.280
<v Maya>So the blocker was a sentence nobody had said.</v>

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:43.340
<v Marcus>The blocker was a sentence nobody had said.</v>

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:49.520
<v Marcus>Once it was said, by the CEO, in front of his team, the whole thing loosened in a week.</v>

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:53.500
<v Marcus>He became the person who migrated his own people across.</v>

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:55.560
<v Marcus>Nobody else could have done that.</v>

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:57.020
<v Marcus>He had the trust.</v>

00:02:57.620 --> 00:02:58.860
<v Marcus>We just had the plan.</v>

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:00.000
<v Maya>Hold on.</v>

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:04.220
<v Maya>You're describing a chief of staff's job as therapy with a timeline.</v>

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:06.600
<v Marcus>I'm describing it as translation.</v>

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:08.980
<v Marcus>The executives speak in outcomes.</v>

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:11.620
<v Marcus>The floor speaks in consequences.</v>

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:17.000
<v Marcus>Somebody has to stand in the middle and carry meaning both ways without losing it.</v>

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:19.720
<v Marcus>The models never mention that person.</v>

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:23.140
<v Marcus>In the textbooks the change just cascades.</v>

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:26.240
<v Marcus>In real life there is no cascade.</v>

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:30.260
<v Marcus>There's a person, walking it from room to room.</v>

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:33.000
<v Maya>Okay, but be honest about the plan.</v>

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:37.100
<v Maya>You had milestones, a steering committee, the whole machine.</v>

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:38.840
<v Maya>Was any of it useful?</v>

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:41.620
<v Marcus>All of it, as long as you know what it's for.</v>

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:44.400
<v Marcus>I keep two versions of every change now.</v>

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:49.740
<v Marcus>The clean one, for the board: the timeline, the milestones, the green boxes.</v>

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:56.520
<v Marcus>And the honest one, that I actually run: the names, the worries, the three things most likely to break.</v>

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:58.760
<v Marcus>The clean version gets you approval.</v>

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:00.980
<v Marcus>The honest version gets you through.</v>

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:02.720
<v Marcus>And the rule is simple.</v>

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:04.760
<v Marcus>Never run the clean slide.</v>

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:06.100
<v Marcus>Maya: Never run it.</v>

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:10.200
<v Marcus>Marcus: Because the moment you start believing the tidy version, you go blind.</v>

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:14.780
<v Marcus>You stop seeing the operations lead in his office until he's a crisis.</v>

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:17.560
<v Maya>Where do the textbooks actually fail, then?</v>

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:18.980
<v Maya>Give me the one place.</v>

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:21.540
<v Marcus>They assume resistance is a phase.</v>

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:25.220
<v Marcus>Step three, manage resistance, move on.</v>

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:27.240
<v Marcus>Resistance isn't a phase.</v>

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:28.460
<v Marcus>It's information.</v>

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:34.240
<v Marcus>It's the organization telling you what the change actually costs the people living it.</v>

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:42.500
<v Marcus>The man in that office was the most honest signal I got in the whole merger, and I'd spent three weeks treating him as an obstacle.</v>

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:44.980
<v Maya>How did you know it was finally real?</v>

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:46.420
<v Maya>Not announced.</v>

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:47.280
<v Maya>Real.</v>

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:50.620
<v Marcus>Two months later we retired his old tracking system.</v>

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:51.940
<v Marcus>The one he'd built.</v>

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.820
<v Marcus>I expected a fight, or at least a funeral.</v>

00:04:55.360 --> 00:05:02.380
<v Marcus>Instead he ran the shutdown himself, and when someone asked for the old report the next week, he said, we don't do that one anymore.</v>

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:04.040
<v Marcus>That's the test.</v>

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:11.040
<v Marcus>The change is real when the old way stops being an option, and the person who loved it most is the one who closes the door.</v>

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:13.540
<v Maya>So the textbooks describe change.</v>

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:15.580
<v Marcus>And the chief of staff delivers it.</v>

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:21.000
<v Marcus>Person by person, conversation by conversation, in rooms the plan never mentions.</v>

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:27.080
<v Maya>If this episode helped, follow the show and tap five stars — it's how other chiefs of staff find us.</v>

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:32.900
<v Maya>I'm Maya, he's Marcus, and this is Chief of Staff Confidential.</v>

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:34.340
<v Maya>See you next week!</v>

